<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benguela</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as São Filipe de Benguela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benguela is a coastal city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, founded by the Portuguese in 1617 as São Filipe de Benguela. It became the second centre of Portuguese Angola after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and a major port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, drawing captives from the central highland kingdoms such as Bailundo and Viye. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> held it briefly in the 1640s. It is now the capital of Benguela province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benguela.jpg" alt="Benguela" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palacio_das_Bolas_(19819765355).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
